11. Security and privacy
FreeStack should be public and not require authentication in its initial release.

Security requirements:
- Treat scraped pages as untrusted input.
- Protect AI prompts against instructions embedded in scraped content.
- Validate all extracted data.
- Restrict automated writes to the intended branches and paths.
- Prevent arbitrary redirects.
- Avoid collecting unnecessary personal information.
- Do not scrape private or access-controlled content.
- Respect provider terms and applicable rate limits.
- Keep credentials out of source control.
- Apply dependency and secret scanning in CI.
12. Testing architecture
Test type	What it checks
Schema tests	Every resource follows the JSON schema
Data integrity	IDs, slugs and links are valid
Unit tests	Individual functions and components
Integration tests	Data loader, search and filters
Pipeline tests	Fetching, normalization and extraction
Verification tests	Evidence and risk classification
Redirect tests	Allowlist and fallback behavior
E2E tests	Real user navigation and search
Build tests	Production build succeeds

Use representative saved page fixtures for extraction tests. This allows pipeline behavior to be tested without repeatedly accessing external websites.
13. Performance and scalability
Initial requirements:
- Generate resource pages statically where possible.
- Optimize images and fonts.
- Avoid fetching resources at request time.
- Keep search and filtering efficient.
- Use pagination or incremental loading when the resource collection grows.
- Cache generated data where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side JavaScript.
As the directory grows, introduce a dedicated search index if Git-based JSON loading becomes inefficient.

Do not add Redis, Kubernetes, microservices or a database until actual requirements justify them.
